{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_a53182f62bcf5bff","type":"tool_result","content":"Diagnosed the 'unknown' gap as an inherited ROCm device-mask contradiction, not a model gap: the harness leaks a PHYSICAL HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES ('2'), Magpie overlays ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 from the yaml without touching HIP, and torch._parse_visible_devices() then raises inside 'import vllm' - so the server dies before binding a port and nothing model-side is ever reached to classify (hence failure_kind=unknown). Authored a 158-line pure-insertion patch to Magpie's _build_local_command that reconciles HIP/CUDA to the LOGICAL 0..n-1 set ROCR re-indexes to (the rule the InferenceX launcher already states but cannot apply to an inherited value), with vendor guard + fail-open so it is inert on CUDA and on already-valid masks. PROVEN END-TO-END: ran the real Magpie serving path under a deliberately leaked parent env with the patch on PYTHONPATH -> logs 'Device-mask reconciliation for local run: HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES: 2 -> 0', weights load, 35/35 CUDA graphs capture, GET /health 200 OK. Cleared the reported launch gap. NEXT DEEPER FAILURE now exposed: the bench CLIENT interpreter /opt/venv/bin/python3 lacks huggingface_hub+transformers (benchmark_lib.sh:682 bare 'python3'), so no inferencex_result.json is produced - a chicken-and-egg gap because the harness installs them only in run_eval, after the client has already died. Also established why attempt 2's patch was likely REVERTED: it targeted /opt/envs/vllm/.../vllm, but the server runs from /opt/envs/wu1w via VLLM_BIN, so such a patch is inert; and an env fix pinning HIP to a same-length physical id passes torch's length check yet yields is_available()==False.\n--- domain enablement_specialist empty False\n--- findings\n * {\"accuracy_risk\": \"none\", \"domain_tags\": [\"enablement\", \"rocm\", \"device-visibility\", \"magpie\"], \"expected_impact\": \"Explains 100% of this session's launch failures; b